description:
SerialShiftBankSwitchStrategy poisons on ANY read-modify-write to the register range:

    Character reg = StoredValueScanner.storeRegister(instr);
    if (reg == null) {
        // A read-modify-write store (INC/ASL/... to the range) isn't a plain byte
        // commit this scanner can attribute a value to; it DID hit the register.
        return poisonAll(inState);
    }

storeRegister() only recognises STA/STX/STY, so INC returns null. Bionic Commando's RESET function (ffe0-ffe6) uses the common MMC1 reset idiom 'INC $FFE1' -- an INC whose operand is its OWN opcode byte -- and gets the 'genuinely undeterminable value' warning, when it is in fact the most determinable write in the program.

WHY IT IS DETERMINABLE: the target is in the FIXED PRG window (PRG_HI c000-ffff, overlay=false in bionic's golden), so the byte is initialised, immutable and unambiguous -- it does not depend on which bank is live. The byte at $FFE1 is the INC opcode $EE, so the write is $EF. Bit 7 SET, which is exactly what the existing reset path wants: applyReset() deposits the descriptor's 'reset: { prg_mode: 3 }' (machines/nes-mmc1.yaml) and clears the shifter. The descriptor ALREADY models this -- its comment even says 'every real MMC1 game's first act is a bit-7-set write, forcing prg_mode=3'. Only the write's VALUE is unrecoverable, not its meaning.

PROPOSED FIX: before the reg==null poison, if the write is an RMW whose target lies in an initialised, non-overlay, read-only block, derive bit 7 of the RESULT from the stored byte and the operation, then fall into the existing bit7Known/bit7Set logic. Per-op: INC = byte+1 and DEC = byte-1 are fully known; ASL and ROL put bit 6 of the byte into bit 7 (known); LSR puts 0 there (known clear); ROR puts the CARRY there (unknown -- keep poisoning). The non-overlay qualifier is load-bearing: in a SWITCHABLE window the byte depends on the live bank, so the read would be a guess.

VALUE BEYOND SILENCING A WARNING: it converts an unknown into a KNOWN prg_mode=3 at the program's reset vector, which is the seed every later inference hangs off. Zelda's/Metroid's quad-reset idiom already works because it uses STA with a bit-7 value; this is the other common spelling.

Found while diagnosing grm-8iy/bionic; reported by the user from their own reversing.

